course: books
lesson: 73
title: The Wind in the Willows, chapter XII, part 4: The Return of Ulysses (Toad's last little song, an altered Toad, and the end of the book)
level: primary-4
stars: 3
explain: Alone in his bedroom before the Banquet, Toad locks the door, draws the curtains, sets the chairs in a half-circle like an audience, and sings «Toad's Last Little Song» — «The Toad—came—home!» — very loudly, twice. Then he heaves a long, long sigh, flattens his hair down neatly, and goes quietly downstairs to his guests.
explain: Everyone cheers him and praises his courage, but Toad only murmurs «Not at all!» or «On the contrary!» When Otter tries to parade him round the room, Toad says gently that Badger was the mastermind, Mole and Rat did the hardest fighting, and he did «little or nothing». No speech, no song, however much the young animals bang the table — just polite talk and questions about their families. Badger and the Rat stare at each other with their mouths open. «He was indeed an altered Toad!»
explain: Afterwards Toad sends the gaoler's daughter a gold chain and locket with pearls and a modest, grateful letter; the engine-driver is thanked and paid for his trouble; and, though Toad grumbles terribly, the barge-woman is paid for her horse. On long summer evenings the four friends stroll through the Wild Wood, and mother-weasels point them out to their babies: the great Mr. Toad, the gallant Water Rat, the famous Mr. Mole — and the terrible grey Badger, who is really rather fond of children.
explain: Looking back over the whole book: a Mole flung down his spring-cleaning and found the River, a best friend in the Rat, the shelter of Badger's home in the snowy Wild Wood, and the sweetness of his own little home again. The Rat heard the piper at dawn and the call of the South, and came back to his river. And Toad went from boats to caravans to motor-cars, prison, disguises and escapes, until friends who never gave up on him won his home back — and he learned, at least for one evening, to let others have the praise.
fact: Before the Banquet Toad «locked the door, drew the curtains across the windows, collected all the chairs in the room and arranged them in a semicircle», and sang to «the enraptured audience that his imagination so clearly saw» (enraptured = filled with delight).
fact: The song begins: «The Toad—came—home! / There was panic in the parlours and howling in the halls, / There was crying in the cow-sheds and shrieking in the stalls, / When the Toad—came—home!» The second verse («There was smashing in of window and crashing in of door») ends the same way. The song has four verses in all.
fact: Another verse: «Bang! go the drums! / The trumpeters are tooting and the soldiers are saluting, / And the cannon they are shooting and the motor-cars are hooting, / As the—Hero—comes!» It ends: «For it's Toad's—great—day!»
fact: He sang it «very loud, with great unction and expression; and when he had done, he sang it all over again» (unction = great feeling). «Then he heaved a deep sigh; a long, long, long sigh.»
fact: He «parted his hair in the middle, and plastered it down very straight and sleek» with water from his hairbrush, and went «quietly» down the stairs.
fact: To all the praise Toad «only smiled faintly, and murmured, "Not at all!" Or, sometimes, for a change, "On the contrary!"»
fact: To Otter: «Badger's was the mastermind; the Mole and the Water Rat bore the brunt of the fighting; I merely served in the ranks and did little or nothing.» (bore the brunt = took the hardest part; served in the ranks = was an ordinary soldier)
fact: When the younger animals cried «Toad! Speech! Speech from Toad! Song! Mr. Toad's song!», Toad shook his head, raised one paw in mild protest, and offered them delicacies (special tasty food) and polite small-talk. Some of the younger and livelier animals whispered to each other that things were not so amusing as they used to be in the good old days.
fact: Badger and the Rat kept «staring at each other with their mouths open; and this gave him the greatest satisfaction». «He was indeed an altered Toad!» (altered = changed)
fact: Toad sent the gaoler's daughter «a handsome gold chain and locket set with pearls» with a letter «that even the Badger admitted to be modest, grateful, and appreciative»; the engine-driver «was properly thanked and compensated» (paid back).
fact: «Under severe compulsion from the Badger» the barge-woman was found and paid the value of her horse; Toad «kicked terribly at this» and still called her rude names — the old Toad had not quite gone.
fact: The horse's price was small, «the gipsy's valuation being admitted by local assessors to be approximately correct» — so the man by the caravan had been right about what the horse was worth.
fact: In the tamed Wild Wood, mother-weasels said: «Look, baby! There goes the great Mr. Toad! And that's the gallant Water Rat, a terrible fighter, walking along o' him! And yonder comes the famous Mr. Mole».
fact: To quiet naughty babies they said «the terrible grey Badger would up and get them» — «a base libel on Badger, who, though he cared little about Society, was rather fond of children» (a base libel = a mean, untrue thing said about someone).
fact: Talk in this lesson: «humble brag» = boasting while pretending to be modest; «main character» = the star; «tara-a-bit» (Black Country) = see you later; «now now» (South Africa) = in a little while; «arvo» (Australia) = afternoon.
exercise: Where did Toad sing his last little song? | Alone in his locked bedroom | At the Banquet | On the lawn | In the boat-house, to the empty boats
exercise: Who was Toad's audience? | Chairs arranged in a half-circle | The guests | The weasels | Mole and the Rat, hiding behind the curtains
exercise: How many times did Toad sing the song? | Twice | Just once | Three times | Ten times, until he was hoarse
exercise: «The Toad—came—home!» What does the song say was in the parlours? | Panic | Dancing | Supper | Flowers and fine new furniture
exercise: What did Toad do just after singing? | Heaved a long, long sigh | Shouted | Laughed | Locked himself in for the whole evening
exercise: How did Toad do his hair? | Plastered down sleek | Curly | Under a cap with motoring goggles on top, ready for a drive | In a bonnet, like the washerwoman
exercise: What did Toad say when guests praised him? | «Not at all!» or «On the contrary!» | «Thank you, I know!» | «Listen to my song, it explains everything I did!» | «Yes, I did it all myself!»
exercise: Who tried to take Toad round the room in triumph? | Otter | The Mole | Badger | The Chief Weasel, to say sorry
exercise: «Badger's was the mastermind.» What does «mastermind» mean here? | The one who made the plan | The strongest | The oldest animal in the whole of the Wild Wood | The teacher of a school
exercise: Who, said Toad, «bore the brunt of the fighting»? | The Mole and the Water Rat | Toad | Otter | The stoats, who fought the weasels
exercise: What did Toad say he himself had done? | «Little or nothing» | Everything | Beaten the Chief Weasel single-handed | Planned it all
exercise: What did the younger animals call for? | A speech and a song from Toad | More food | Dancing on the tables till morning | Otter to tell his stories
exercise: How did Toad answer their calls? | Shook his head and raised one paw | Sang loudly | Left the room | Made a long speech about his prison
exercise: What were Badger and the Rat doing whenever Toad looked at them? | Staring at each other, mouths open | Laughing | Eating cold chicken and trifle as fast as they could | Asleep in their chairs
exercise: «He was indeed an altered Toad!» What does «altered» mean? | Changed | Very angry | Tired out | Taller, after plastering his hair
exercise: What did Toad send the gaoler's daughter? | A gold chain and locket set with pearls | A song | A motor-car, painted red, with his name on it | Toad Hall's best china tea-set
exercise: What did even Badger say about Toad's letter to her? | It was modest, grateful and appreciative | It was far too long and full of boasting | It was boastful | It had spelling mistakes
exercise: Who else was thanked and paid back? | The engine-driver | The magistrates | The Chief Weasel | The ferret at the gate
exercise: Why was the barge-woman paid for her horse? | Badger made Toad do it | Toad offered gladly | The police ordered it | She sued him in the court
exercise: How did Toad feel about paying her? | He «kicked terribly» | He was pleased | He didn't mind | He paid twice the price
exercise: Whose valuation of the horse turned out to be about right? | The gipsy's | Toad's | The barge-woman's | Badger's, who knew horses well
exercise: Where did the four friends stroll on summer evenings? | In the Wild Wood | In Town | On the downs | Along the canal past the barges
exercise: What did mother-weasels call the Water Rat? | Gallant, a terrible fighter | Silly | Small and frightened of everyone in the Wild Wood | The poet of the river bank
exercise: Whom did mother-weasels use to frighten naughty babies? | The terrible grey Badger | Toad | Mole | The policemen from the town
exercise: What was the truth about Badger and children? | He was rather fond of them | He hated them and chased them away | He ate them | He never saw any
test: Daisy, «humble brag» alert: «Toad's 'I did little or nothing' is fake, he's boasting!» Best answer from the book? | He enjoys seeming modest, but gives the credit away | He boasts all night | He sings anyway | He never speaks
test: Why does Toad sing the song alone in his bedroom? | He promised no songs at the Banquet | He likes the echo | He's practising for the Banquet | Rat asked him
test: Is it TRUE or FALSE? «Toad sang his song at the Banquet in the end.» | False: he kept his promise | True: he sang it twice | True: Otter made him | False: there was no Banquet
test: What does the «long, long, long sigh» show? | Goodbye to his boasting | He is tired | He is bored | He is ill
test: Leo: «the guests were dead happy Toad went quiet, it was well better than the old days.» Put it right. | Some young ones missed the old fun | Yes, all of them | Nobody came | The guests left early
test: Why did Toad's new behaviour give him «the greatest satisfaction»? | He enjoyed surprising Badger and Rat | He hated them | He was hungry | He'd won a bet
test: What would the gaoler's daughter have thought of her present? | That Toad was grateful | That it was stolen | That it was a joke | That it was from the police
test: Why was paying the barge-woman fair, even though Toad grumbled? | He had stolen and sold her horse | She had helped him | She asked nicely and wrote him a polite letter | She was his aunt
test: Nan, tara-a-bit (see you later): «so Badger's scary to kids, then.» What does the book say? | No: he is fond of children | Yes, he gets them | He eats babies | He has no friends
test: Looking back: in chapter I, what was the very first thing the Mole did in the book? | Spring-cleaning | He rowed a boat | He met Toad | He sang
test: Looking back: in chapter II, what did Toad want instead of boats, before the motor-car? | A gipsy caravan and «the open road» | A house-boat | A train of his own, with a railway | A horse
test: Looking back: in chapter V, what home did the Rat help Mole find again? | Mole End | Toad Hall | Badger's house | A new hole by the river
test: Looking back: in chapter VII, whom did Rat and Mole find asleep at the feet of the Piper at dawn? | Little Portly, the baby otter | Toad | A weasel | A hedgehog who had lost its way to school
test: Looking back: in chapter IX, who nearly made the Rat leave the river and go South? | The Sea Rat | The swallows only | Toad, in his motor-car | The wise old Badger
test: Looking back: in chapter VI, Toad said he'd jump into «the very first motor-car I see». How is this ending different? | He keeps a promise | He buys a car | He drives to the Banquet | He breaks it at once
test: Looking back: in chapter I, the Rat said of the Wide World beyond the Wild Wood, «I've never been there, and I'm never going». Who in the book went out into the wide world, and came home again? | Toad | The Mole | Badger | Portly
test: ★★★ Count the verses of «Toad's Last Little Song» that end «When the Toad—came—home!», «As the—Hero—comes!» and «For it's Toad's—great—day!». There are four verses; two end the same way. Which line ends two of them? | «When the Toad—came—home!» | «As the—Hero—comes!» | «For it's Toad's—great—day!» | «Shout—Hoo-ray!»
test: ★★★ Toad sang the four-verse song twice. How many verses did he sing in all? | 8 | 4 | 6 | 2
test: ★★★ «I merely served in the ranks and did little or nothing.» Which of these is TRUE from the battle chapter? | Toad did fight the Chief Weasel | Toad really did nothing | Toad led the attack | Toad stayed in the passage
exam: Identify the manner in which Toad comported himself in response to the congratulations of his guests. | He murmured «Not at all!» | He delivered an address upon Our Prison System | He sang his own compositions | He withdrew from the company
exam: Ascertain to whom Toad attributed the «mastermind» of the recapture of Toad Hall. | The Badger | The Mole alone | The Water Rat | Toad himself
exam: It is given that recompense was made to several persons. Select the response which correctly identifies the gift dispatched to the gaoler's daughter. | A gold chain and locket set with pearls | A sum of six shillings and sixpence | A motor-car | A washerwoman's gown
exam: Which of the following statements is consistent with the established facts concerning the reputation of the Badger among the mother-weasels? | They used his name to frighten infants, though he was fond of children | They regarded him as their protector | They were unaware of his existence | They invited him to their banquets
